---
interface Props {
  id?: string;
  class?: string;
  variant?: 'default' | 'highlight';
  /* 'screen' is the tool-page rhythm: each card is a full-viewport section
     scrolled to below the app, so its content sits centred in its own screen.
     'flow' is for prose pages that are nothing but cards - there the 100vh
     floor turns three paragraphs into a screenful of empty space and makes
     the page read as padded out rather than substantial. */
  density?: 'screen' | 'flow';
}

const { id, class: className = '', variant = 'default', density = 'screen' } = Astro.props;

const baseClasses = [
  // Block padding (top/bottom) comes from the fluid --space-section-block
  // token (DEMO-01) instead of the old fixed 56px-desktop/40px-mobile pair
  // of vertical-padding utilities, so a card's own vertical padding scales
  // continuously rather than snapping at one breakpoint. Horizontal padding
  // stays a plain Tailwind step - it is not part of the vertical-rhythm
  // system this token drives. (Deliberately not spelling out the two old
  // utility class names here: Tailwind's source scanner reads .astro files
  // as prose too, and a class-shaped string in a comment compiles into the
  // build as a dead rule - see CLAUDE.md, "Tailwind source scanning".)
  'box-border w-full max-w-[1080px] mx-auto rounded-[var(--radius-lg)] px-10 space-section-block',
  'last:mb-16',
  'max-[1080px]:mx-6 max-[1080px]:w-auto',
  'max-[640px]:px-6',
  'min-[768px]:last:mb-24',
  // Scroll-reveal hook - see the <style> below, and index.astro, which both
  // publishes a view timeline from this class for the card parallax and
  // switches the reveal off inside its story-card stack.
  'card-reveal',
];

const densityClasses = density === 'screen'
  ? 'mt-16 min-[768px]:mt-24 min-[768px]:flex min-[768px]:min-h-[calc(100vh-6rem)] min-[768px]:flex-col min-[768px]:justify-center'
  : 'mt-6 min-[768px]:mt-8';

const variantClasses = variant === 'highlight'
  ? 'border border-[var(--color-primary-tint)] bg-[linear-gradient(135deg,var(--color-primary-soft)_0%,var(--color-bg)_100%)]'
  : 'border border-[var(--color-border)] bg-[var(--color-surface)]';
---
<section id={id} class:list={[baseClasses, densityClasses, variantClasses, className]}>
  <div class="w-full">
    <slot />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* The settle-into-place reveal, driven by the card's own view progress
     timeline: the card's opacity is a pure function of where it currently
     sits in the viewport, so there is no event to miss and no "has this been
     revealed yet" state that can be wrong.

     That is the whole reason this is no longer an IntersectionObserver. An
     observer only ever learns about states some rendered frame actually
     passed through, and a single-step scroll - the End key, a scrollbar
     drag, a `scrollIntoView()` - moves a card from below the viewport to
     above it with no frame in between where it was ever intersecting. The
     callback then never ran at all, so the card kept the opacity: 0 the
     script had given it and stayed invisible for good, while still taking up
     its full height: a screen-tall blank gap where the content should be.
     That shipped, and read on /sign/ as a huge gap between "Free for
     everyone" and the cards below it.

     Two guards, matching the parallax block in index.astro:

     - @supports. Everything is inside it, the hidden start state included,
       so a browser without scroll-driven animations renders these cards
       exactly as it would with no CSS here at all: fully visible, no
       transform. The hidden state cannot outlive the mechanism that undoes
       it - which is precisely the guarantee the script could not make.
     - prefers-reduced-motion, as everywhere else on the site.

     `view()` measures the card against the page even though the card is
     itself `overflow-hidden`, and therefore a scroll container: a view
     timeline's scroller is the nearest *ancestor* scroll container, and a
     card is not its own ancestor. A decor layer *inside* a card is the case
     that does need the named timeline index.astro publishes. */
  @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .card-reveal {
        animation-name: card-reveal-settle;
        animation-duration: auto;
        animation-timing-function: linear;
        animation-fill-mode: both;
        animation-timeline: view();
        /* Ends the fade 15% into the card's entry, which is ~150px of
           scrolling and, importantly, roughly that for every card on the
           site: the `entry` range is capped at the scrollport's height, not
           the subject's, so 15% of it is one figure whether the card is one
           viewport tall or the FAQ card's three. A share of `cover` would
           not be - that one does scale with the card, and the same 150px
           costs 8% on one card and 4% on another.

           Short on purpose. The fade is done while the card is still showing
           about its own top padding, so the copy inside it is never on
           screen at a partial opacity - which matters because a scroll-linked
           animation has no duration of its own: a visitor who stops mid-range
           stops the fade there, and text parked at 40% opacity would miss the
           contrast floor for as long as they sit there.

           Do not write this as a bare length (`entry 0% 15vh`). It parses,
           and it silently means something else: the length is read as the
           range *start*, leaving the end at `entry 100%` - a ~750px fade that
           does exactly what the paragraph above rules out. */
        animation-range: entry 0% entry 15%;
      }
    }
  }

  /* Referenced only from inside the feature query above, so on a browser
     without scroll-driven animations nothing plays this and the from-state
     never applies. */
  @keyframes card-reveal-settle {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(14px);
    }

    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
  }
</style>
